# The git half of the build identity, `<version>+<commit>[-dirty]`: one rule,
# read by both builds that compute it, so that a UI built with the binary
# carries the identity that binary reports. bin/ragondin/build.rs reads it for
# the binary, ui/scripts/build-identity.mjs for the UI's bundle.
#
# Each non-comment line is the arguments of one git command, split on
# whitespace, run at the repository; a command that fails or prints nothing
# yields nothing. The first prints the commit: nothing means `unknown`, and
# then the tree is never dirty. The second prints something when the tree is
# dirty.
#
# Dirty is a tracked file modified, or a change staged, against HEAD. An
# untracked file is not: no build reads one it was not told about (a Finder
# `.DS_Store`, an editor's swap file), and counting it made the binary say
# dirty where the page did not, so the page refused to load. A new source
# file is dirty once staged; until then, a build reaches it only through an
# edit to a tracked file that names it, and that edit is dirty.
# `--no-optional-locks`: a plain `status` may rewrite the index, which the
# binary's build script watches, and would rerun it for a change it made.
rev-parse --short=12 HEAD
--no-optional-locks status --porcelain --untracked-files=no
